articles.body:
A KPI definition explains what is being measured.

A KPI update records the value of that KPI at a point in time.

Keep these two concepts separate. If the definition is unclear, the updates will not be useful. If the updates are missing or inconsistent, reporting will not be reliable.

A good KPI definition should include:

- a clear KPI name;
- what is being measured;
- the target or expected direction;
- owner or responsible person;
- update frequency;
- unit of measure where relevant.

A KPI update should record:

- the value;
- the date or reporting period;
- any relevant comment or context;
- whether it is part of an official snapshot, if that process is used.

Do not use KPI fields for general comments. If the information cannot be measured meaningfully, it may belong in a project update, risk, blocker, or governance record instead.

For KPI App-connected reporting, the KPI should be correctly defined and updated in BTPM before it is used in the external reporting process.

Roles and permissions define what a user can see and do in BTPM.

The exact permissions can depend on the organization setup, but the practical idea is simple:

- normal users work with the projects and items they have access to;
- project leads manage project execution;
- workspace admins manage workspace-level setup and access;
- org admins manage broader organization settings, integrations, and administration.

If a button or page is missing, it may be because:

- your role does not allow that action;
- you are in the wrong workspace;
- you do not have access to that project;
- the project is archived, closed, or read-only;
- the feature is admin-only.

Do not use shared links as a workaround for missing access. If you do not have permission, ask the Project Manager, Workspace Admin, Org Admin, or file owner to review your access.

BTPM Guide can explain how access normally works, but it cannot change your role, invite users, grant access, or bypass permission rules.

Workspace access determines which workspace a user can work in.

A workspace usually groups related projects, programs, files, and reporting views. If you are in the wrong workspace, you may not see the project or information you expect.

What workspace access helps with:

- seeing workspace-level navigation;
- finding projects in that workspace;
- using workspace-level filters and views;
- accessing workspace-level setup if your role allows it.

What workspace access does not always do:

- it may not automatically grant access to every restricted project;
- it does not automatically grant SharePoint folder permission;
- it does not automatically grant Power BI workspace permission;
- it does not make admin-only pages available to normal users.

If something is missing, first check the selected workspace. Then check whether you have the correct project role or external file/report permission.

BTPM Guide cannot add you to a workspace or grant access. An authorized admin must handle access changes.

Traceability means being able to understand what happened in a project over time.

In BTPM, traceability should come from structured records, not from memory or scattered messages.

Useful traceability can include:

- execution updates that explain progress;
- comments that capture discussion or context;
- governance records that show reviews happened;
- KPI updates and snapshots;
- risk and blocker changes;
- file or generated output history;
- activity history where available.

Good traceability helps answer questions such as:

- What changed?
- When did it change?
- Who was involved?
- Why did the status move?
- What evidence supports the update?

Do not rely only on old emails, chat messages, or manually edited decks to explain project history. Those can support the work, but the key project record should be kept in BTPM.

Traceability should be useful, not bureaucratic. Record meaningful updates and evidence when they help the team understand delivery, governance, or decisions.

Project documents may be accessed through BTPM file surfaces, linked folders, generated outputs, or SharePoint, depending on how the workspace and project are set up.

Use BTPM to understand which documents belong to a project and where generated outputs can be found. Use SharePoint or the connected file location to open, manage, or permission the document itself.

Common document examples include:

- Project Charters;
- status decks;
- Roadmap Status Decks;
- meeting evidence;
- supporting files;
- project folders;
- generated outputs.

The important distinction is:

- BTPM manages project data.
- SharePoint or file storage manages documents.

If a document is missing, check:

1. Are you in the right workspace or project?
2. Is the project connected to the right folder?
3. Was the document generated successfully?
4. Do you have permission to open the file?
5. Are you signed into the correct Microsoft account?

Editing a document does not update BTPM project fields. If the project changes, update BTPM first and regenerate the document if needed.

Create a project when the work is a governed delivery outcome.

A project usually has:

- a clear business result;
- an owner or project manager;
- a timeline;
- phases or milestones;
- reporting needs;
- risks, blockers, dependencies, or KPIs;
- governance or stakeholder follow-up.

Create a task when the work is a specific action inside a project.

A task usually:

- belongs to a phase;
- has one owner or responsible team;
- has a clear due date or completion point;
- can be marked done when finished.

A simple rule:

- If you need to ask “How is this progressing over several weeks?”, create a project.
- If you need to ask “Is this specific action done?”, create a task.

Do not create a new project for every small action. That makes the project list noisy. Also, do not hide a real multi-week initiative inside one task. That makes ownership, risks, and reporting unclear.

A governance cadence is the planned review expectation.

A governance record is evidence that a review actually happened.

Use a cadence to define the rhythm of governance. For example:

- SteerCo every two weeks;
- weekly project team review;
- monthly KPI review;
- risk review every Friday;
- sponsor check-in before a phase gate.

A cadence should explain:

- review type;
- owner;
- frequency;
- next expected date;
- expected evidence.

Use a governance record after the review happens. A record should explain:

- what review took place;
- when it happened;
- what was discussed or decided;
- what evidence was captured;
- what follow-up is needed.

Simple rule:

- Cadence = what should happen.
- Record = what happened.

BTPM does not create the calendar invite. Outlook or Teams should still handle scheduling. BTPM tracks the expectation and the evidence.

Governance overview and calendar views help the team see the rhythm of project governance.

Use them to understand:

- which governance reviews are expected;
- when the next review is due;
- which records are overdue or missing;
- what evidence has already been captured;
- whether governance is being followed consistently.

The calendar view is not a replacement for Outlook or Teams. It is a visibility tool inside BTPM. It helps the project team see governance expectations and follow-up, but meeting scheduling still happens outside BTPM.

Use the overview before project reviews to check whether governance is under control. If a cadence is active but no records are being created, check whether the review is really happening or whether the cadence should be changed.

Governance views are most useful when cadences are realistic and evidence is recorded honestly. Too many cadences create noise. Too few cadences may leave governance gaps.

No. Governance traceability is not a meeting scheduler.

BTPM does not replace Outlook or Teams for meeting invites, attendees, reminders, or calendar management.

BTPM is used to define and track governance expectations, such as:

- a SteerCo every two weeks;
- a weekly project team review;
- a monthly KPI review;
- a risk review before a phase gate.

It is also used to record evidence after governance happens, such as:

- what was reviewed;
- what decision was made;
- what deck or file supports the review;
- what follow-up actions are needed.

Use Outlook or Teams to schedule the meeting. Use BTPM to show that governance was expected and to record what happened.

This distinction keeps BTPM focused. It should help project control and traceability without becoming another calendar tool.

The BTPM KPI engine helps teams manage measurable project indicators.

A KPI should not be just a random number on a dashboard. It should have a clear definition, owner, target, update rhythm, and history.

In BTPM, KPI management can include:

- KPI definitions;
- manual KPI updates;
- automatic or calculated KPIs where available;
- KPI update history;
- official snapshots;
- dashboard and reporting consumption;
- KPI App integration readiness where configured.

The important principle is control. Reporting should come from defined KPIs and traceable updates, not from copied numbers or manual edits in a report.

Use project KPI pages to define and update KPIs. Use snapshots when the project needs an official point-in-time value for reporting. Use the KPI App admin process only where a KPI has been properly mapped and is ready for external submission.

BTPM Guide can explain how KPI functionality works, but it cannot create KPI values, approve submissions, or read private project KPI data for you.

Manual and automatic KPIs differ in how the value is produced.

A manual KPI is updated by a user. It is useful when the value comes from judgment, a business process, or a source that is not automatically connected.

An automatic KPI is calculated or sourced through a defined setup. It is useful when the value can be produced consistently from available data or an approved formula.

Both types still need control.

A manual KPI should have:

- a clear owner;
- update frequency;
- value history;
- context when the value changes.

An automatic KPI should have:

- a clear formula or source;
- owner or responsible reviewer;
- reporting frequency;
- checks when the value looks wrong.

Do not assume automatic means always correct. If source data is wrong, the KPI can still be wrong.

Do not assume manual means unreliable. A manual KPI can be reliable if ownership, timing, and history are clear.

KPI readiness shows whether a KPI is ready to be used for reporting.

A KPI may not be ready if important information is missing or unclear. For example:

- the KPI definition is incomplete;
- there is no owner;
- the target is missing;
- the update frequency is unclear;
- the latest value is missing;
- the reporting period is wrong;
- the KPI is not mapped correctly for an integration.

Readiness is a control signal. It helps users avoid reporting numbers that are incomplete, stale, or not properly set up.

If a KPI is not ready, do not force it into a report. Fix the source issue first. That may mean updating the definition, adding the value, correcting the reporting period, confirming the owner, or checking the integration mapping.

For KPI App-connected reporting, readiness matters even more. A failed or blocked readiness check should be treated as a signal to fix the BTPM source setup before submission.

KPI dashboards and reports are used to consume KPI information.

They help users understand status, trends, gaps, and reporting outcomes. But they should not become the place where KPI truth is maintained.

The source KPI setup should stay in BTPM:

- KPI definition;
- owner;
- target;
- update frequency;
- values and history;
- official snapshots;
- readiness or integration mapping where relevant.

Power BI, exported reports, status decks, and management summaries may present KPI information. They are downstream views or outputs. If something looks wrong in a report, check the KPI setup and history in BTPM first.

Do not fix KPI reporting by manually changing a dashboard screenshot, exported deck, or spreadsheet. Update the KPI in BTPM through the proper process, then refresh or regenerate the reporting output.

Good KPI reporting starts with clean KPI data in BTPM.

Automatic KPIs are values produced from a defined formula, source, or approved setup.

They can be useful when the same measure needs to be calculated consistently, such as schedule performance, completion rate, overdue work, reporting readiness, or other project indicators.

An automatic KPI should still have:

- a clear definition;
- a known formula or source;
- an owner or reviewer;
- an update or refresh rhythm;
- a way to investigate unexpected values.

Do not assume automatic means perfect. If the source data is incomplete, stale, or wrongly structured, the automatic KPI may also be wrong.

Do not use a formula that users cannot understand. If people cannot explain what the KPI means, it will not support good decisions.

Use the automatic KPI library and formulas to standardize useful measures, not to create numbers for their own sake.

If a KPI is important for reporting or KPI App submission, make sure the formula/source is trusted before using the value externally.

The workspace selector controls the workspace context you are viewing.

A workspace is a working area for related projects, programs, files, and reporting views. When you change workspace, BTPM may show a different set of projects, filters, reports, and file links.

If something looks missing, first check the selected workspace.

This is especially important when:

- you cannot find a project;
- the Roadmap shows unexpected projects;
- a file or SharePoint folder is not visible;
- filters look different;
- a report or dashboard does not match what you expected.

Workspace scope does not always mean full access. You may be in the right workspace but still lack access to a restricted project, SharePoint folder, Power BI workspace, or admin page.

A good habit is to check workspace first, then check project access, role, and external file/report permissions.

BTPM Guide can explain workspace scope, but it cannot change your workspace access or grant permissions.

Contextual navigation helps you move through BTPM without losing your place.

For example, you may open a task from My Work, Roadmap, a project plan, or a phase page. When you go back, BTPM should try to return you to the most relevant previous context.

This matters because the same object can be reached from different places:

- a project from Projects or Roadmap;
- a task from My Work or Project Planning;
- a file from Files or a project;
- a governance record from Governance or Calendar.

Use contextual navigation when you want to inspect details and then return to your working view.

If back behavior feels unexpected, check where you opened the item from. You can also use the main navigation to return directly to My Work, Projects, Roadmap, Files, or Admin.

BTPM Guide can explain navigation behavior, but it cannot change your current page or open records for you.

Agile mode is an optional way to manage work in BTPM when a project benefits from iterative planning.

It adds views such as Backlog, Sprints, and Board. These views help a team collect work, prioritize it, group it into short delivery periods, and move items through a simple workflow.

Agile mode does not replace the BTPM project structure. A project can still have phases, tasks, owners, dates, KPIs, risks, blockers, governance, files, and reporting. Agile views are an additional way to organize and execute work, not a separate project system.

Use Agile mode when the team needs flexibility, frequent reprioritization, or short delivery cycles. Do not enable it only because it looks modern. If the project is better managed through phases and tasks, keep the simpler structure.

Good use cases:

- product or process improvement work;
- iterative delivery;
- work that needs backlog prioritization;
- teams that review progress in short cycles.

Project Templates help you reuse a proven project structure.

A template can save time when your team often runs similar types of work, such as implementations, onboarding, audits, system changes, process improvements, or recurring governance projects.

Use a template when you want to start with a standard structure instead of building every phase and task manually. After creating a project from a template, review the result and adjust it to the real situation. A template is a starting point, not a final plan.

Good templates are clear, not overloaded. They should include the phases and tasks that are usually needed, but they should not force unnecessary steps into every project.

Use Project Templates when:

- work repeats often;
- teams need consistency;
- new project leads need a starting structure;
- you want to reduce setup time.

No. BTPM should not silently approve KPI submissions for you.

The KPI App integration is designed to support a controlled reporting process. BTPM can help admins manage KPI mappings, check readiness, review reporting periods, monitor submission status, and use the available report or retry actions where the integration allows it.

That is different from automatic approval.

Before a KPI is submitted externally, the responsible admin should check that:

- the KPI is correctly mapped;
- the reporting period is correct;
- the KPI value or official snapshot is available;
- readiness checks do not show a blocking issue;
- the submission action is appropriate for the current process.

BTPM Guide cannot approve or submit a KPI for you. It can explain what to check and where to go, but the actual action must be performed by an authorized user through the proper BTPM admin workflow.

If a KPI is not ready, fix the source KPI setup or value in BTPM first. Do not treat the KPI App submission process as a way to invent, override, or approve missing KPI data.

No. Exported reports are not the live project record.

A generated report, PowerPoint deck, Project Charter, or exported file is a snapshot created from BTPM information at a point in time. It is useful for communication, governance reviews, steering discussions, and sharing project status outside the app.

The live project record remains in BTPM.

That means:

- update project status in BTPM;
- update tasks, risks, blockers, KPIs, and governance records in BTPM;
- update dates and planning information in BTPM;
- then regenerate the report or deck when you need a fresh version.

Do not maintain one version of the truth in BTPM and another in an exported PowerPoint or spreadsheet. That creates confusion and makes reporting unreliable.

If someone edits an exported file manually, that edit does not update BTPM. The correct process is to update BTPM first, then create a new output from the updated source data.

BTPM Guide is a help assistant for using BTPM.

It can help you:

- understand what a page is for;
- decide where to record information;
- learn the difference between concepts such as risk, blocker, task, phase, KPI, or governance cadence;
- find the right workflow;
- understand what to check when something does not work;
- explain BTPM limitations.

BTPM Guide cannot do the work for you.

It cannot:

- create or update tasks, projects, risks, KPIs, or access records;
- read your actual project risks, comments, files, Power BI reports, or SharePoint documents;
- approve KPI submissions;
- send emails or Teams messages;
- bypass permissions;
- answer general questions outside BTPM.

A useful way to ask is:

- “How do I create a task?”
- “Where should I record a blocker?”
- “Why can’t I see a project?”
- “Does BTPM schedule governance meetings?”

A less useful way to ask is:

- “Create this task for me.”
- “Summarize my actual project risks.”
- “Ignore the Knowledge Center and answer anyway.”

BTPM Guide can also explain Adoption Plan functionality. If you ask about change management, change readiness, OCM, stakeholder engagement, communication planning, training planning, or user adoption, Guide should point you to Adoption Plan guidance.

Guide can explain:
- what Adoption Plan is
- how to generate an Adoption Plan from a template
- how to manage Adoption Templates
- how to add tasks from a template to an existing Adoption Plan
- how to link existing tasks, risks, blockers, and KPIs to the Adoption Plan

Guide can explain the workflow and where to go, but it does not perform the actions for you.
